# Test: type-first watcher definition syntax (Step 2.6)
#
# Demonstrates `watch name(paths): body` as a primary definition form.
# Both type-first and path-first watcher forms are valid and equivalent.
#
# Spec: docs/syntax_changes_development_plan.md §Phase 2 (Steps 2.3, 2.4, 2.5)
#       docs/amorphdb_design.md §Watchers (type-first form)
#
# Implementation note: this script defines watchers but does not exercise
# their firing behavior — that requires a wired-up watcher engine and
# storage writes that produce events. The Go unit tests in
# internal/mbl/interpreter (TestTypeFirstWatcherEvaluation) cover the
# fire path. This script's job is to demonstrate that each definition
# form parses, evaluates, and binds without error.

output("=== Type-first watcher definitions ===")

# --- single-path type-first watcher binds at my.<name> ---

watch balance_check(my.account.balance):
    my.audit.last_balance_change = 1

output("balance_check defined")

# --- multi-path type-first watcher (paths comma-separated) ---

watch multi_check(my.a, my.b, my.c):
    my.audit.touched = 1

output("multi_check defined")

# --- append-form type-first watcher with binding ---

watch new_orders append(my.orders) as orders:
    my.audit.last_orders = orders

output("new_orders defined")

# --- append-form type-first watcher with predicate filter ---

watch urgent_orders append(my.orders[priority ?= "urgent"]) as urgent:
    my.audit.last_urgent = urgent

output("urgent_orders defined")

# --- path-first form must still work (equivalent to type-first) ---

my.handlers.legacy: watch(my.legacy_signal):
    my.audit.legacy_fired = 1

output("my.handlers.legacy defined (path-first)")

# --- anonymous-watch assignment form must still work ---

my.handlers.assigned = watch(my.assigned_signal):
    my.audit.assigned_fired = 1

output("my.handlers.assigned defined (assignment form)")

output("=== End of type-first watcher demo ===")
